# The USB audio adapter's identity, as a stable systemd device unit.
#
# WAS 90-wall-line-in.rules. It started the line-in passthrough when the
# adapter appeared (2026-09-12), because a plain
# ConditionPathExists=/proc/asound/ICUSBAUDIO7D raced USB enumeration and lost:
# after a reboot the unit was skipped with "start condition unmet" and the
# passthrough was absent until someone started it by hand. Device activation is
# still the fix, and it still lives here.
#
# WHAT 2026-09-13 ADDED (Owner item 25): the hub was moved to another port. The
# adapter came back with the same card id, but the three long-running audio
# consumers kept handles on the dead device instance and never noticed. The
# units now BindsTo= the alias below, so a re-enumeration STOPS them, and the
# SYSTEMD_WANTS on the way back in STARTS them again.
#
# ENV{SYSTEMD_ALIAS} IS THE WHOLE POINT AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL. The kernel's own
# device unit name embeds the USB path --
# sys-devices-...-usb1-1\x2d2-1\x2d2.4-...-sound-card1.device -- and that name
# CHANGES when the hub moves ports, which is the exact event being recovered
# from. The alias is a port-independent name for whichever port it is on now.
# /dev/wall_audio_adapter is a name, not a node: nothing creates that file, and
# systemd only turns it into dev-wall_audio_adapter.device. Underscores, not
# dashes: a dash in the path is escaped to backslash-x2d, and every unit file
# would then have to spell the dependency that way by hand -- one lost
# backslash and the BindsTo points at a unit that can never exist.
#
# NO ACTION=="add" ON THE TAG OR THE ALIAS. Measured on the panel: card1 showed
# TAGS=:systemd:seat: but CURRENT_TAGS=:seat: -- a later non-add event had
# reprocessed the rules without the add-only line, so the systemd tag was stale
# SWITCH-DEPENDENT LEGS ARE NOT NAMED HERE. This used to grow a stale list:
# adding the rear mic fixed one hub move, but bus mode later added the two
# speaker halves and neither was restarted. `wall-audio-resume.service` is the
# non-sticky re-assert of `apply-state`; it consults ALL_LEGS in
# wall_audio_state.py, the one policy owner of speaker/headset/mic legs. The
# direct wants below are only always-on or legacy-mode consumers outside that
# policy. Keep the rule an activation mechanism, never a second switch plan.
#
# `wall-bus-visualizer.service` IS named here, and it is not an exception to the
# paragraph above: it is not a switch leg. It reads `bus_monitor` regardless of
# where the switch is pointing, so ALL_LEGS does not and must not know about it,
# and without a direct want a hub move would leave the wall with no visualizer
# until the next mode switch or reboot. Its own ExecCondition still keeps it out
# of the modes that have no such PCM (SR-041, LLR-957).
#
# and the device unit was not tracked at all. udev re-evaluates every rule on
# every event and a property not set by THIS event does not exist, so the tag,
# the alias and the wants are all set on everything except remove.
ACTION=="remove", GOTO="wall_audio_adapter_end"
SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="card*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0d8c", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0102", \
    TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_ALIAS}="/dev/wall_audio_adapter", \
    ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="wall-line-in.service", \
    ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="wall-kiosk-loop.service", \
    ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="wall-spdif-in.service", \
    ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="wall-amp-trigger.service", \
    ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="wall-bus-visualizer.service", \
    ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="wall-audio-resume.service"
LABEL="wall_audio_adapter_end"
